[Music]  welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Nason adil par VAR who is a  postdoctoral associate and lecturer in  the program in Iranian studies at the  Council on Middle East Studies through a  rice faculty fellowship professor Adil  var pars primary research interests  around the nature of social relations  and conflict-affected and transitional  contexts he currently works with the  UNDP  in Iraq to engage minorities and  vulnerable groups and political and  legislative processes today we'll talk  with him about the changing nature of  ethnic and religious identity in  Afghanistan's Bamian Valley welcome  professor thank you very much it's good  to be here let's start with an overview  of your research tell us about it  so my research focuses in essence on  political reconstruction in post 2001  Afghanistan and the impacts for  intergroup relations so I did my  fieldwork for the research in Bamiyan  which is a valley in the central  highlands area of Afghanistan and it's  also the essential ancestral home sorry  for the Hazara community the Hazara are  an ethnic Shia minority but there are  two other major communities there one is  the target community which is an ethnic  Sunni community and also the saudade  community and they are descendants of  the Prophet Muhammad and that gives them  a particular religious charisma which  they used to enter into like interrelate  with the government between the Hazara  community and the government and also  more generally in terms of religious  leadership in that community so they are  seen to have more prestige because of  that connection traditionally and  typically that's the case okay so why  Afghanistan what led you to be  interested in this region of the world  well I my heritage is one of the reasons  that kind of brings me to that mind my  father's Iranian my mother thing  I grew up in the UK but essentially one  of my interest was to work overseas in  that part of the world generally  speaking which took me to Afghanistan  and I went there initially to work with  the UN in about 2006 and I spent two or  three years working with the UN and  small local organizations on various  development interventions but one of the  things that I found is that as security  deteriorated my engagement in the  engagement of the internationally as a  whole was very limited with our Afghan  counterparts so became frustrating  policies based on assumptions about  what's happening and I wanted to kind of  overcome that and doing research on the  ground for an extended period of one way  of doing that and then really as I  started to look at that kind of research  process the sociological and  anthropological literature on  Afghanistan it became clear that  ethnicity was one entry point and given  my own background my mixed heritage it  was something that initially I I  connected with and I really wanted to  explore so it's both a professional and  personal journey when you're doing the  research I would imagine there's not a  lot of written work to draw from is that  correct  and then I'd like to have you talk a  little bit about how your academic work  has contributed to the field  sure yeah you're absolutely right  there's the literature particular and  identity in Afghanistan is very  fractured it's very outdated because  limited research has been done since  1970s not 79 when the conflict started  in the country with the Soviet  occupation so there are some pieces of  work which do talk about the Hazara  community for example but there's very  little little there really to build upon  so what I wanted to do was to contribute  academically and empirically to that  that gap as it were and I think there  was three things that I really wanted to  talk to you the first was the literature  on national integration and power  sharing and what I really wanted to do  is take the the ships that took place in  Afghanistan after the Constitution was  ratified in 2004 and look at those  implications as it comes down to local  levels to individuals to those  communities that I mentioned earlier was  one area  the second is really to talk  specifically to the Afghanistan specific  academic literature that conceptually  it's very fragmented it's quite dated so  trying to draw together that literature  the concepts  present a new idea or state-of-the-art  kind of thinking on ethnicity an  identity and then empirically the third  area is to document what's going on and  as I said earlier there are there are  some pieces of literature which look at  the Hazara community but I wanted to  look at contemporary changes areas for  violence areas for for tension how  that's happening on what basis is  happening to really kind of open that  discussion up and what years are you  looking at again well the field work was  done 2010 to 12 as part of my PhD but  I'm going back even to the end of the  19th century starting there looking at  the formation of ethnicity and identity  but more post 2004 but particularly at  that period that I did my fieldwork okay  so you had mentioned the I know there's  chronic insecurity in Afghanistan so  when you're doing your field work during  that two-year time period tell us about  that were you ever concerned and I know  you also were working with a variety of  other students to to assist you in doing  your work so tell us about that field or  process well it's obviously not an easy  place to do work that was one of the  reasons that I selected Bamiyan was not  only because of the ethnic diversity but  because it's one of these safest  provinces in Afghanistan you know we're  just getting access to do that it was a  five-month ethical clearance process  with the University which was  unprecedented for my colleagues at the  time and there's two main concerns that  one was primarily about the safety of my  research participants those I was  interviewing and also my safety so we  took some of the typical academic steps  in terms of informed consent making sure  that people knew the danger they were  placing themselves in which clearly they  did and also anonymizing information  making sure it was securely stored but  in terms of actually doing the research  the the main way that we thought the  main way that my security was ensured  was I was hosted by an international  organization that has a  office there I stayed in their  guesthouse which thankfully is in the  center of one of the communities that I  was working with but did post some  challenges in terms of isolating me from  that community and I was subject to  security information coming from the  organization so on three occasions I can  think of I was unable to go to the  community or the village that I wanted  to go to because of some kind of  smoldering conflict or some tensions  that had been perceived or kind of  through kind of intelligence or  gathering information so we had to kind  of reorganize the schedule but generally  speaking it it unfold without any major  problems which is always good right  that's great so when you're in doing  your fieldwork did you were there any  surprises that you just did not  anticipate yeah I think one of the big  ones you mentioned about the students I  was working with and they were from a  local university University of Pavia  one of the first interviews I remember  doing was going to interview a member of  the saudade community and he was quite a  important figure and I went with my two  Hazara colleagues one that was  translating for me of supporting and  translation and one that was note-taking  and it was a really awkward and  comfortable kind of encounter which I I  kind of cut short and afterwards I was  kind of talking to you you know my  colleagues about what exactly went wrong  and it became obvious very quickly that  because we're talking about Hazara  saudade relationships it's political  it's sensitive it was uncomfortable  because of the team I was working with  so what I ended up having to do was to  work with three teams two men and two  women from each of those ethnic  communities so I had a team of twelve in  the end simultaneously doing research so  once we got around that obstacle it was  then a case of actually managing the  volume of work that was going on and  making sure it was transcribed correctly  which was intensive but produce a lot of  really good data at the end okay and  tell me about tell me about some  specific examples of tensions that are  that you attract while you're there well  I looked at two main case studies  between the as IRA Tariq dimension and  the Hazara so dot dimension and in the  the the latter case the as we mentioned  nearly the Saadat community have this  let's say  religious superiority or at least  perceived that way because of  historically the role they've played in  the Hazara community and the they've  always taken wives from the Hazara  community often a second wives but never  given their daughters to be married to  them as our community so it really  exemplifies that power relationship but  what's happening now is that has our  community with an increasing status  that's come from the way the state has  reorganized and allocate resources in  Bamiyan they started to say well why  can't we Mary there's nothing  religiously that stops us it's just a  tradition so there's increase in cases  of elopement taking place now in Bamiyan  in which has are a man run away with  society women but the soldered community  retaliates very violently to try and  maintain that relationship and although  there are political reasons this is one  of the interfaces at which the two  communities are really kind of coming  apart and differ a check differentiating  themselves and I've tried to document  that that process on the flip side  looking at the target community I've  looked very much at morning rituals and  one in particular which is Ashura which  really looks at the oppression that the  Shia community in this case that has our  community has faced historically at the  hands of Sunni communities of the Sunni  State and the way it's articulated to  you so we find very much that somewhat  through Iranian influence in terms of  the way these rituals are played out  you're seeing an increasing tension  being driven between these two sectarian  groups and this has happened after 2004  once the Constitution recognized the  ability for the Shia community to  practice their rituals so again I've  tried to demonstrate and look at that  cleavage as it kind of opens up between  those communities okay and what do you  conclude ultimately well I think there's  three main things I conclude the first  is really looking at the very earth Nick  nature in which the state is now  operating since 2001 and again that goes  back to the Constitution the way the  government has been structured the way  resources are allocated and that really  shows that this has become a modus  operandi of the way the government  functions the way that people make  claims or communities make claims  against the state for greater security  more resources or services  and the way that the president  particular appeals to those communities  to try win votes and then saying that  because there's this kind of ethnic  nature of politics ethnic identities  become increasingly salient or prominent  in everyday life for better or for worse  and personally I don't think you know  increasing social differentiation is a  good thing so I don't think that long  run that's going to have a great impact  on Afghan Society secondly I think it's  also important to recognize that this  process of political reconstruction or  development more generally is actually  or potentially a violent process as I  mentioned earlier these changing  relationships between groups when you're  you know challenging old inequalities  can result in violence and I think we  need to understand that better I think  we need to work to mitigate that better  both in terms of the state and the  international community and the last  area which is more a general set of  findings that my research outlines is  really just understanding the way that  ethnicity or ethnic identity functions  in the case of Bamiyan and to show how  that functions and to understand better  the need for political analysis and for  the workings and everyday settings which  is something that really hasn't been  done adequately in the context of  Afghanistan and on that note will you be  pursuing a further research there on  those issues yeah Zak actually were  hosting a conference again in April  which will start to open up those issues  about identity not just ethnic identity  but other forms of identity which could  range from the impact of returnees  coming back and having effect on older  and younger generations and the way that  that is deployed or functions in Afghan  society there's a whole host of areas  who hope to hope to open up and to  publish on in the coming year or so okay  well thank you so much for being here  today and think about your work thanks  for more information about professor  adler Parv r and his work please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies  yeah  you

